description:
Spec: draft-atwood-jmap-chat-00 §Message senderExpiresAt + burnOnRead.

Spec text:
- "Message.senderExpiresAt in the past at delivery time: MUST reject with invalidArguments." (verify current behavior; likely already enforced)
- "When senderExpiresAt fires: MUST hard-delete (row removal, not tombstone) and message MUST appear in destroyed of subsequent /changes."
- "burnOnRead: receiving server MUST hard-delete immediately after setting readAt; receipt-sharing-off still fires the local hard-delete"

Current state: senderExpiresAt is stored. burnOnRead is stored. There is NO firing path — no scheduler in the kit, and no hard-delete hook on Message/set update that sets readAt while burnOnRead is true.

## Acceptance

- New ChatBackend method:
  ```rust
  fn expire_message(
      &self,
      _caller: &Self::CallerCtx,
      _account_id: &Id,
      _message_id: &Id,
  ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<(), BackendSetError<Self::Error>>> + Send {
      // Default: do nothing. The kit does not run a scheduler;
      // production backends invoke this from their own timer infrastructure
      // (e.g. for senderExpiresAt) or from a Message/set readAt+burnOnRead handler integration.
      async { Ok(()) }
  }
  ```
- handle_message_set on update path: when the patch sets readAt AND the existing message has burnOnRead: true, the handler calls expire_message(caller, account_id, message_id) AFTER applying the readAt patch. The hard-delete is the backend's job.
- MemoryBackend overrides expire_message to actually remove the message from storage and record it in the destroyed-set for Message/changes.
- Test: MemoryBackend + send message with burnOnRead: true + recipient sets readAt → message is gone from get; appears in destroyed of subsequent Message/changes
- Test: MemoryBackend + senderExpiresAt in the past on create → reject with invalidArguments (verify existing behavior continues working — chat-server/src/message.rs already has this at lines 390-397, just regression-test it)

## Out of scope

- Building a timer/scheduler for senderExpiresAt firing — that's deployment territory. The kit just exposes expire_message; the consumer's scheduler calls it.
- receivedAt-side hard-delete (handled the same way the consumer plugs in expire_message)
- Notification fan-out on hard-delete (transport-layer; consumer)
